\f1\fs22 Sarah and Chanta sat on an elevated dais overlooking the crowd, two thrones that might once have graced a particularly ambitious burlesque production. Sarah\'92s emerald gown, expertly draped, concealed the small swell of her belly\'97fifteen weeks now, barely showing but impossible to ignore when she shifted position. A dull ache had settled in her lower back an hour ago and wasn\'92t budging.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Beside her, Chanta radiated like a small sun gone supernova. The Elvi had outdone themselves: his gold jumpsuit (the one that had previously attempted to blind him) now sported a floor-length cape of red velvet studded with enough rhinestones to fund a small nation. Across his chest, a sash proclaimed 
\fs18 GREAT TEACHER
\fs22  in letters that caught every stray photon. On his head, a crown\'97an actual crown\'97fashioned from what appeared to be partly melted Elvis bobbleheads.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93They won\'92t let me take the sunglasses off,\'94 Chanta muttered without moving his lips, smile frozen in place for the adoring crowd below. \'93Said they\'92re sacred now.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You look like a disco ball had a baby with a Medieval king,\'94 Sarah whispered back.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93That\'92s exactly the look I was going for.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A moment of almost-comfortable silence. The kind they used to share in the mansion, before PAPER, before Eloise, before everything got complicated.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93How are you?\'94 Chanta asked, still not looking at her directly. \'93Really?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s hand drifted to her belly before she could stop it. \'93Tired. Backache. But strangely, my sex drive is back.\'94 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta dared turn sunglasses towards Sarah. She blinked against the light from the rhinestones. \'93Oh, ah\'85 no,\'94 back-pedalled. \'93I\'92m in no state to be cashing in on at it at the present time.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Even even in his outfit, Sarah could see Chanta\'92s shoulder fall.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>She paused. \'93I\'92m marrying Alexander in three months.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I heard.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It\'92s\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You don\'92t have to explain.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>But she wanted to. Wanted to tell him about the Random Allocation trap, about Project Kebab, about the dreams with patterns burned into walls. Wanted to ask if he still saw that golden glow around her belly that he\'92d mentioned once, half-drunk, before the mansion attack.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Instead: \'93How\'92s the messiah business?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta snorted, then quickly converted it into a solemn nod for an Elvisette who was openly weeping at his feet. \'93Terrible. I accidentally started an atheist religion. And I still can\'92t write lyrics worth a damn.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93The \'92Happy Dawkins\'92 song?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93You heard about that?\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Hayley has sources.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Another silence. Less comfortable this time.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I miss you,\'94 Chanta said quietly. Simple. Direct.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah felt something crack in her chest. \'93I miss you too.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93But\'97\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'94It\'92s impossible.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Yeah.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>What else was there to say? He was the Great Teacher, channeling the words of the Dawkins. She was marrying into the most powerful family in Ulyssia, while a government agency wanted her arrested. Wanted them both arrested.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The crowd began to surge. The High Elvis was approaching the centre of the room, preparing to speak. Sarah tightened her pelvic floor muscles and tried not to think about how long this ceremony might last. She was confident she could endure the occasion. She was less confident about her bladder. She should have accepted Kathy\'92s offer of incontinence pants.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Here we go,\'94 Chanta said.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shifted in her throne, trying to ease her back, and watched as the High Elvis climbed onto what she now realised was definitely a spa bath.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Before the building was Parliament House, Chanta recalled, it used to be a brothel called Clambake. Looking around at the faded grandeur, the name made perfect sense.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The room where Time had once thrown a party, then left without cleaning up. Shy light, as if embarrassed by the room\'92s decline, came down in blurred puddles. Tarnished chandeliers hung overhead, their cut-glass dulled by years of disregard. Patches of wallpaper, once as vibrant as a rose garden at high noon, peeled and drooped.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A once-proud bar slouched along one wall, its mahogany top worn smooth; an expansive mirror, spotted and worn, reflected the room like an old vaudeville performer squinting through cataracts. Ghostly decanters stood guard, their spirits long evaporated, on dusty shelves.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Heavy curtains, the colour of a cabaret singer\'92s lipstick, hung faded and moth-eaten against the wall. The grand but bruised staircase, its balustrade as intricately carved as a Victorian cameo, spiralled up to a mezzanine of secret rooms hidden behind doors that sighed under the weight of their own stories.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The air was thick with the ghost of yesteryear\'92s perfume, lingering in the room like a stubborn rumour that refused to be silenced.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The crowd occupying most of the ground floor was a riot of leather, cowboy outfits, army uniforms, once-glamorous evening dresses, and sequinned jumpsuits shimmering like disco balls caught in a cyclone. In the eye of the storm, a figure in a suit studded with twinkling rhinestones rose a little above the floor, standing atop the room\'92s belle of the ball\'97a spa bath with faded mosaics decorating its bottom, like the bleached tattoos of an ancient sailor, depicting mermaids that seemed more world-weary than whimsical. The fixtures, once gleaming gold, had traded their lustre for the hue of an old pirate doubloon.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93My fellow Elvi,\'94 the High Elvis said, his voice boomed despite sinking into the tired furnishings. \'93Praise the Dawkins.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The crowd returned: 
\f2\i Praise the Dawkins! Bless the Holy Momma!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah felt Chanta shift uncomfortably beside her. She resisted the urge to reach for his hand.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I am all shook up by the honour you bestow upon me. My hands are shaking and my knees are weak from the thought of such a fool as I being gifted this pocket full of rainbows. I have a burning love for all of you in the Ghetto. Suspicious minds may think I have taken this role to be the big boss man, but you are always on my mind and I love you so. As we travel along the long Jericho road\'97cross my heart and hope to die\'97we can make the morning and you\'92ll never walk alone. What we need is a little less conversation and a little more action so I washed my hands in muddy waters and they came out clean. It is no secret it\'92s a wonderful world and you\'92re the reason I\'92m living. If you give me all of your love, I\'92ll never stand in your way and there will be peace in the valley. I am pledging my love and let me be the one, but only the strong survive so please don\'92t stop loving me.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>He paused and the adoration of the Elvi spilled over into deafening applause and cheers.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93It is time we put aside our differences. It\'92s time we reclaimed our sense of pride, and our self-determination. It\'92s time for deeds and actions. We must begin with good works in our own home, the Ghetto, first.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah\'92s back throbbed. She shifted again, trying to find a position that didn\'92t make her spine scream.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93Even though we rarely see the sun here under the Layer, in the shadows, under the great Airborne towers, we will create light in our hearts. Our spirits will rise from the shadows of the Ghetto and even from the Pale too, may their hopes and dreams shine brightly. We will make our home a place where families can visit without fear of robbery, rape or murder.\'94\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>High Elvis! High Elvis! Praise the Dawkins! Bless the Holy Momma!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93I propose we take a simple, immediate, actionable step towards regaining our dignity,\'85\'94\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>High Elvis! Elvis!
\f1\i0  the crowd chanted.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'94\'85 to channeling our undoubted charm and charisma,\'85\'94\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>High Elvis! Praise the Dawkins!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93\'85 to saying no to all the Tom Parkers of this world\'85\'94\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Tax the Tom Parkers! High Elvis! Tax the Tom Parkers! Praise the Dawkins!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta leaned slightly toward Sarah. \'93I have no idea who Tom Parker is,\'94 he whispered. \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah shrugged. \'93I doubt they know either.\'94\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93\'85 and to owning our talent. We are strong, and handsome, and beautiful, and virile. There is nothing we cannot achieve.\'94.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The crowd began losing the contents of their chitlins, or their shit, whichever you prefer.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We will take our music to the rest of Ulyssia and on to the rest of the world!\'94\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>We\'92ll be famous! We\'92ll be richer than the Airborne!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93My fellow Elvi, we must have self-respect, we must have dignity\'85\'94\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Self respect! Dignity!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah tightened her pelvic floor again.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>For the first time in many decades we will revive communities, we will give people some pride and offer more than mere hope. Look around you, my fellow Elvi; the ground you stand upon is a rotting sludge of dirt, garbage and shit. We are all forced to live in this festering cesspool of filth and crime. We have been served up nothing but contempt, but we will rise above it all.\'94\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Filth and Crime! Filth and Crime!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93With a single act, we can show the world we are no longer jokes, no longer just clones of a long-dead entertainer; we are serious.\'94\

\f2\i <$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Long live long-dead entertainers!
\f1\i0 \
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Chanta winced. Sarah caught it\'97just the slightest tightening around his eyes behind those sacred sunglasses.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>\'93We will remove the filth; we will collect the rubbish; we will scrub the footpaths, hose down the streets, and shovel up the shit. We will clean up the Ghetto!\'94 the High Elvis proclaimed.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>The chanting ceased. A silent pall fell among the crowd.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>Sarah and Chanta both leaned forward.\
<$ScrKeepWithNextSplittable>A lone voice from the crowd called, \'93We\'92re musicians, not street sweepers.\'94
